        <mxCell id="pat" value="How it works" style="text;html=1;fontSize=15;align=left;fontColor=#9673a6;fontStyle=1;verticalAlign=top;whiteSpace=wrap;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1470" y="196" width="1010" height="26" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="pab" value="IDENTITY IS PRESENTED, NEVER CLAIMED. The relay verifies the token at CONNECT, mints a NATS user-JWT scoped to that sub, and sets ckp.requester transaction-locally before ckp.dispatch runs. The seal path reads the GUC. Nothing in the payload can influence it.  WHY Agent AND Participant ARE DIFFERENT. onBehalfOf is present when an agent sealed FOR a participant, and absent when the participant acted directly. That is how an executor writing to a kernel it does not own is attributed — rather than by a caller-supplied &quot;workspace&quot; field, which is exactly the forgeable pattern this replaces.  A PERMISSION TARGETS AN ORGAN, NOT A KERNEL. Grant → permTarget → Organ is what makes the Separation Axiom enforceable by write-authority instead of by convention: DATA cannot write CK because the grant does not reach it.  MEASURED. Before 0.4.44 a single seal carried TWO identities — ownedBy from the verified connection, createdBy from the payload, which defaulted to anon:. &quot;Owned by someone, created by nobody.&quot; The verified connection now wins and a conflicting payload sub is ignored, never merged.  THE DELEGATION SEAM EXISTS AND IS UNUSED. Delegation is fully declared, ckp.affordance_registry has a delegate column, and dispatch already calls it &quot;the Tier-2 tool seam&quot;. What is missing is only the routing edge that would make SomeKernel.Info() resolve to another kernel." style="text;html=1;fontSize=27;align=left;fontColor=#333333;fontStyle=0;verticalAlign=top;whiteSpace=wrap;" parent="1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1470" y="232" width="1010" height="690" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
