        <mxCell id="Module" value="Module  the adoptable unit (the module IRI IS the graph IRI)" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;fontSize=13;fontStyle=1;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="660" y="190" width="340" height="140" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="composed" value="&lt;project&gt;/shapes/composed  core  +  kernel graph  +  every ADOPTED module materialised once, here — not at validate time" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;spacingLeft=16;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="100" y="480" width="900" height="140" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="failclosed" value="FAILS CLOSED  If an adopted module graph is absent or empty, composition RAISES. A vanished module silently narrowing the gate is un-enforcement nobody would ever see." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#f8cecc;strokeColor=#b85450;fontSize=12;align=left;spacingLeft=16;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="100" y="680" width="900" height="140" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="ef1" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=1;html=1;endArrow=open;strokeColor=#d6b656;strokeWidth=2;" edge="1" parent="1" source="Adoption" target="Module">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="ef1L" value="adopts BY DIGEST" style="edgeLabel;html=1;fontSize=11;fontColor=#d6b656;labelBackgroundColor=#FFFFFF;" vertex="1" connectable="0" parent="ef1">
          <mxGeometry x="0" relative="1" as="geometry">
            <mxPoint as="offset" />
          </mxGeometry>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="ef2" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=1;html=1;endArrow=open;strokeColor=#d6b656;strokeWidth=2;dashed=1;" edge="1" parent="1" source="Module" target="composed">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="ef2L" value="joins the surface" style="edgeLabel;html=1;fontSize=11;fontColor=#d6b656;labelBackgroundColor=#FFFFFF;" vertex="1" connectable="0" parent="ef2">
          <mxGeometry x="0" relative="1" as="geometry">
            <mxPoint as="offset" />
          </mxGeometry>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="pf" value="" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#d6b656;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1450" y="190" width="1050" height="830" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="pft" value="How it works" style="text;html=1;fontSize=15;align=left;fontColor=#d6b656;fontStyle=1;verticalAlign=top;whiteSpace=wrap;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1470" y="206" width="1010" height="26" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="pfb" value="THE DIGEST IS THE POINT. An Adoption names sourceDigest — 64 hex — so &quot;which wave module am I enforcing?&quot; has an answer that survives the file changing under you. The shipped modules carry pass-stamped .sha256 sidecars; core, wave and lexicon all match theirs byte for byte, and the release tarball now ships the sidecars so a consumer can check what it received rather than trust the tarball.  A FILE ON DISK IS NOT AN ENFORCEMENT SURFACE. Loading a graph into the store is not adoption either. Only a sealed Adoption puts a module into the composed shapes — which is why a kernel can hold the wave vocabulary in its store and still be unable to seal a wave:Ticket.  COMPOSITION IS MATERIALISED ONCE. core + the kernel graph + every adopted module are copied into &lt;project&gt;/shapes/composed and the OWL closure is computed THERE — because pgrdf.validate does not entail, and entailment is per-graph. Skipping it makes a malformed body conform VACUOUSLY, which is the failure this whole design is arranged against.  MEASURED — THE SURFACE IS REAL AND MEASURABLE. A composed surface on this bench: 1258 triples, 27 sh:targetClass. The kernel graph alone: 30 triples, ZERO targets. Validating against the wrong one of those two is not a small error — it is the difference between a gate and a decoration, and ckp.validate_instance was doing exactly that until 0.4.45.  ADOPTION IS PER-PROJECT AND PER-EPOCH. intoProject and intoEpoch mean a kernel can adopt wave at epoch 3 and another kernel never adopt it at all, and both statements stay true and queryable." style="text;html=1;fontSize=12;align=left;fontColor=#333333;fontStyle=0;verticalAlign=top;whiteSpace=wrap;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="1470" y="242" width="1010" height="760" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
  <diagram id="w" name="wave.ttl — coordination">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1800" dy="1200" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="2600" pageHeight="1500" math="0" shadow="0">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" />
